# Liberico Coding Rules

**LibeTech (BU7) engineering governance** — áp cho mọi phần mềm LibeTech phát triển, bất kể pháp nhân sở hữu (Liberico BU · IBS HI · Fidelitas). Scope chốt 2026-09-18; FA / tổ chức / nhân sự / an toàn ngành theo từng pháp nhân, KHÔNG gộp. Owner: Chairman (quyết) · LibeTech BU7 (thực hiện). Platform — **BTNET** (Fastify+Vue wholesale distribution), **Xeca** (multi-tenant B2B SaaS for bus operators), **LBROS/Libe-OS** (Next.js+Supabase internal governance), **Fidelitas Brain** (Python data pipeline). **The engineering core is stack-neutral** (ADR-018 D2): E-BE/E-DB/E-INT/E-SEC/E-OBS/E-TEST apply to every stack. Technology-specific rules live in stack profiles — `references/stack-fastify-vue.md` (BTNET), `references/stack-nextjs.md` (LBROS), `references/stack-multi-tenant.md` (Xeca/LBROS), `references/stack-python-data.md` (Fidelitas Brain). A React/Next.js repo follows the Next.js profile and is **not** subject to the Vue rules E-FE-01..03.

## Role boundary — read this first

This skill has host-specific execution modes:

- **Builders (Codex or Claude Code)** — generative when the user asks to build or change software in an authorized repository. Apply the rules as constraints on implementation, tests, migrations, and reviews. Codex follows the applicable `AGENTS.md`; Claude Code follows the applicable `CLAUDE.md`. If the other host's instruction file contains relevant project facts, read it explicitly rather than assuming it was auto-loaded.
- **Cowork / advisory sessions** — analyze, flag violations, draft or review ADRs, audit a spec/PRD, check Quality Gate readiness, and explain rule codes. Do not turn an advisory request into an unrequested production-code change.

Either way, the rules themselves don't change — only what you *do* with them does.

**Out of scope**: IBS HI heavy-industry engineering (structural calculations, weld specs, Tekla BIM, marine classification) is a separate corp with its own Brain and its own safety-critical validation chain (PE/CWI/QA sign-off) — do not apply AR-XX/E-XX-XX software rules there. Also out of scope: general AI-agent session behavior (COMMIT_PROTOCOL Hard Gate, R1-R15, trigger-word interrupts) — that governs how Cowork *operates*, not how code is written, and lives in COWORK_GLOBAL_RULE / AI Brain, not here. `references/ai-governance.md` is bundled for the narrow AR-XX "what AI-generated code must look like" slice only.

## The 3 canonical documents + how they relate

```
Architecture Principles (DESIGN) → Engineering Standards (CODE) → Quality Gate (REVIEW)
        AR-01 → AR-10                  E-BE / E-DB / E-FE /            PR checklist
        Z1-Z4 zones                    E-INT / E-EXT / E-SEC /         Migration safety
        Canonical entities             E-OBS / E-TEST / E-AI           Release process
        Integration patterns           + CLAUDE.md template            CI enforcement
```

Every repo should expose project-specific instructions to its active coding host: `AGENTS.md` for Codex and `CLAUDE.md` for Claude Code. These files record stack choices, data-layer names, tenant column, folder structure, and scope; this skill defines the group-wide constraints those choices must obey. Use `assets/CLAUDE-md-template.md` as the maintained project-facts template. For Codex onboarding, adapt those project facts into a concise `AGENTS.md` and point to this skill instead of duplicating the full rulebook.

## Quick reference — check these before opening a reference file

Most questions can be answered from this table alone. Open a `references/` file only when you need full detail (code examples, full checklist, ADR template, exact wording for a document you're drafting).

### AR-01 → AR-10 — Hard Rules (immutable; violation requires ADR + Chairman approval)

| # | Rule |
|---|------|
| AR-01 | No direct DB queries into another platform's database |
| AR-02 | Market/scraping data never feeds the pricing engine directly |
| AR-03 | AI/Intelligence layer writes only to Z4 — never Z1/Z2 |
| AR-04 | Python defaults to data pipeline/AI. A Python service MAY own its domain logic **only with an accepted ADR** naming its bounded context, the zone it writes, and its integration path (ADR-018 D2) |
| AR-05 | External data must carry all 4 source-tracking fields |
| AR-06 | Multi-tenant isolation testing must be 100% before go-live |
| AR-07 | Analytics/reporting queries must **never** hit Z1 — at any hour. The old "peak hours only" wording was retired 2026-08-02 (ADR-018 D1) because it contradicted the Zone table and E-DB-10 |
| AR-08 | Enrichment/transformation pipelines must be codified (no manual/Excel) |
| AR-09 | Every canonical entity has exactly 1 owner platform |
| AR-10 | No integration pattern beyond the 3 allowed ones without an ADR |

Full rationale + consequences: `references/architecture-principles.md` Section VII.

### Z1–Z4 data zones

| Zone | Purpose | Writes | Reads |
|------|---------|--------|-------|
| Z1 Operational | Real-time business data | Owning platform | Owning platform only |
| Z2 Analytics | KPIs, cross-platform reports | ETL pipeline | Dashboards, BI, AI |
| Z3 Historical | Long-term archive, audit, ML training | Batch job | Archive queries |
| Z4 Intelligence | AI signals (forecast, scoring) | AI pipeline (write-only) | Platforms (read-only) |

Redis/cache is infrastructure serving Z1, not a zone of its own. AI signal loop: **Z4 → platform shows suggestion → human approves → platform writes Z1.** AI never writes Z1/Z2 directly (AR-03). Full detail + data-flow diagram: `references/architecture-principles.md` Section II.

### 3 allowed integration patterns

REST API (sync request/response) · Message Queue (async, event-driven) · ETL Pipeline (batch, cross-zone). Anything else needs an ADR (AR-10). DB-to-DB queries across platforms are never allowed regardless of pattern.

### Database standards (non-negotiable, every new table)

PK = `UUID DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()` (no SERIAL/INTEGER) · `created_at`/`updated_at` = `TIMESTAMPTZ` (no bare TIMESTAMP) · money = `NUMERIC(15,2)` (no FLOAT/DOUBLE) · schema changes only via versioned migration files, never direct ALTER · multi-tenant tables need a tenant column + isolation test. Full list: `references/common/database.md` (quick) or `references/engineering-standards.md` Section 2 (full, with SQL examples).

### Testing coverage gates

Tenant isolation tests: 100% · domain logic unit tests: ≥60% · API integration tests: ≥40% · every bug fix ships with a regression test. Full: `references/common/testing.md`.

## Reference map — when to load what

| Question type | Load |
|---|---|
| New feature/platform design, "does X need an ADR", canonical entity, data zone placement, integration pattern choice | `references/architecture-principles.md` |
| Specific `E-BE-XX`/`E-DB-XX`/`E-FE-XX`/`E-INT-XX`/`E-EXT-XX`/`E-SEC-XX`/`E-OBS-XX`/`E-TEST-XX` rule lookup, code-pattern examples, drafting a new repo's project instructions | `references/engineering-standards.md` (project-facts template at `assets/CLAUDE-md-template.md`) |
| PR checklist, migration safety (expand/migrate/contract), release/rollback process, CI enforcement targets, reviewer guide | `references/quality-gate.md` |
| Quick cross-cutting lookup: coding style/naming, git commit format & branching, security/PII checklist | `references/common/coding-style.md`, `references/common/git-workflow.md`, `references/common/security.md` |
| "What must AI-generated code look like" (repo structure, no new architecture, Python scope, AI signal pattern) | `references/ai-governance.md` Section 1–3 only — do not use this file for session-behavior questions (see Out of scope above) |

`references/common/architecture.md` and `references/common/database.md` and `references/common/testing.md` are condensed one-page versions of material also in `references/architecture-principles.md`/`references/engineering-standards.md` — use the `common/` version for a fast lookup, the full doc when you need to quote or cite exact wording.

## Common workflows

**"Does this violate our rules?"** — Check the Quick Reference table first. If it's a clear AR-01→10 hit, say which rule, cite it, and note that any change needs an ADR (Chairman sign-off) rather than silently allowing or blocking. Don't guess on ambiguous cases — pull the exact rule text from `references/architecture-principles.md` or `references/engineering-standards.md` before concluding.

**Drafting or reviewing an ADR** — ADRs are required for: new platform, changing any AR-01→10 rule, new canonical entity, new integration pattern, changing a data-zone assignment, changing data ownership (full trigger list in `references/architecture-principles.md` Section VIII). Use the ADR template there (Context → Decision → Consequences → Alternatives considered). An ADR is a decision record, not a request for permission — draft it as a recommendation with a clear decision, ready for Chairman to approve or reject.

**Auditing a spec/PRD before Chairman review** — Walk it against: Z1-Z4 zone assignment for any new data, canonical entity reuse vs. new entity (AR-09 needs exactly 1 owner), which of the 3 integration patterns it uses, and whether external data sources declare all 4 source-tracking fields (AR-05). Flag gaps as questions for the spec author, not as blockers you resolve yourself.

**Quality Gate / PR readiness check** — Walk the PR checklist in `references/quality-gate.md` Section I by category (Backend/Database/Frontend/Cross-cutting/Testing). For schema changes, additionally check Section II migration safety (expand→migrate→contract, no dropping without ≥2 sprints deprecation). Note MUST vs SHOULD severity — SHOULD items need justification to skip, not silent omission.

**Onboarding a new platform or repo** — Fill in STACK/DATA LAYERS/MULTI-TENANT/EXTERNAL SOURCES/CANONICAL EXTENSIONS/DATA OWNERSHIP/FOLDER STRUCTURE/SCOPE from `assets/CLAUDE-md-template.md`. Save the result as `CLAUDE.md` for Claude Code. For Codex, create a concise `AGENTS.md` with the same verified project facts plus an instruction to use `$liberico-coding-rules`; do not paste the group-wide rules into both files.

## Freshness note

This skill's `references/` are a point-in-time bundle of `00_CHAIRMAN/RULES/` on Google Drive, synced **2026-09-18** (scope LibeTech + sweep V7→V8.2 + bỏ số COMMIT_PROTOCOL cứng; ADR-018 lineage). Distribution to Claude Code (`~/.claude/skills`) and Codex (`~/.agents/skills`) is hash-verified by `AI/sync-liberico-rules.sh`; drift is caught by `AI/verify-governance.sh` and `AI/manifest-rules.py --check`. The Chairman's Drive folder is the canonical source — if a rule looks like it might have changed since (new version header, a rule number that doesn't match what the Chairman describes), say so explicitly rather than asserting the bundled copy is current, and suggest re-syncing this skill from Drive.

